   "story": "The farmers' markets that now fill Prague's Saturdays are recent: Kulaťák has run on its Dejvice roundabout only since 2010. Náplavka and Jiřák, on the river and in Vinohrady, bring producers from across Bohemia and Moravia into the city. What they sell is a short, cold-climate larder with strong opinions: rye and caraway, cabbage and mushrooms, pond carp, smoked pork, a stinking curd cheese from one Moravian town, plum butter, and a half-fermented wine that by law exists only from August to November. The Holešovice hall, built as the city slaughterhouse in the 1890s, is the working version; SAPA, the Vietnamese market in a former meat plant on the southern edge, is the city nobody puts on a postcard.",
