   "title": "The chlebíček and the bread ban",
   "story": "The Czech open sandwich on a slice of veka, the chlebíček, is credited to the Prague delicatessen owner Jan Paukert, around 1916, reportedly after his friend the painter Jan Skramlík asked for something between a canapé and a sandwich. The Czech Wikipedia entry flags the problem: rationing began in September 1915, and a November 1916 Prague ordinance banned serving bread in establishments, so the neat founding year sits awkwardly with the war. The original Paukert on Národní, famous for its salami-and-egg chlebíček, operated until February 2015. The dish outlived its origin story; it is now the standard office party food of the whole country.",
